#!/bin/sh
# mission [N]  prints a mission brief, the same text as the panel on the page.
# Generated by guest/make-missions.py: edit that file, not this one.
case "$1" in
  0) cat <<'EOF'
Mission 0: Warm-up   [optional, 50 bonus points, untimed]

Objective: Read the CTF orientation in the terminal with cat.

  cat orientation.txt

Get comfortable before the clock starts. Your home folder holds
orientation.txt: how a CTF works, what a flag looks like, scoring and
the rules.

cat prints a file on the screen. Click the terminal, type cat
orientation.txt and press Enter. The warm-up flag is at the bottom.

Submit it for 50 bonus points, or skip the warm-up. The competition
clock starts when you begin Mission 1.

When you have the flag: submit CYBA{...}     Stuck? hint 0
EOF
  ;;
  1) cat <<'EOF'
Mission 1: Base camp   [Easy, 100 points]

Objective: Recognise base64 and decode it.

  cd ~/mission1
  cat message.b64

Encoding is not encryption: it writes the same data a different way, and
anyone can undo it. No key needed.

message.b64 is written in base64: upper and lower case letters, digits,
+ and /, often ending in =. Decode it to read the flag.

Useful commands: cat, base64
When you have the flag: submit CYBA{...}
Stuck? hint 1   (1 hint, 5 points each: 5% of this mission)
EOF
  ;;
  2) cat <<'EOF'
Mission 2: Hex marks the spot   [Easy, 100 points]

Objective: Turn hexadecimal back into text.

  cd ~/mission2
  cat signal.hex

Computers store everything as numbers. Hexadecimal (hex) writes each
byte as two characters from 0-9 and a-f: the letter A is 41 and a space
is 20.

signal.hex is a message written in hex. The xxd tool converts between
text and hex: read its help to run it in reverse.

Useful command: xxd
When you have the flag: submit CYBA{...}
Stuck? hint 2   (1 hint, 5 points each: 5% of this mission)
EOF
  ;;
  3) cat <<'EOF'
Mission 3: Spin cycle   [Easy, 100 points]

Objective: Undo a ROT13 rotation.

  cd ~/mission3
  cat note.txt

ROT13 rotates every letter 13 places along the alphabet: A becomes N and
B becomes O. The alphabet has 26 letters, so rotating by 13 twice brings
you back where you started.

note.txt was rotated with ROT13. Rotate it again to read it. This
computer has a caesar tool for shifting letters.

Useful commands: caesar, tr
When you have the flag: submit CYBA{...}
Stuck? hint 3   (1 hint, 5 points each: 5% of this mission)
EOF
  ;;
  4) cat <<'EOF'
Mission 4: Hail Caesar   [Medium, 200 points]

Objective: Break a Caesar cipher by trying every shift.

  cd ~/mission4
  cat scroll.txt

Julius Caesar hid messages by shifting every letter a few places.
scroll.txt uses a Caesar shift, but nobody wrote down which one.

There are only 25 possible shifts. A computer can try them all in a
blink: trying every possible key is called a brute-force attack.

Useful command: caesar
When you have the flag: submit CYBA{...}
Stuck? hint 4   (2 hints, 10 points each: 5% of this mission)
EOF
  ;;
  5) cat <<'EOF'
Mission 5: Russian doll   [Medium, 200 points]

Objective: Peel three layers of encoding, one at a time.

  cd ~/mission5
  cat doll.txt

Like a set of Russian dolls, this message is wrapped in three layers:
hex, base64 and ROT13. Working out the order is part of the puzzle.

Look at the text, decide which layer is on the outside, decode it, then
look again. Pipes (|) chain the steps into one command.

Useful commands: xxd, base64, caesar
When you have the flag: submit CYBA{...}
Stuck? hint 5   (2 hints, 10 points each: 5% of this mission)
EOF
  ;;
  6) cat <<'EOF'
Mission 6: Fingerprints   [Medium, 200 points]

Objective: Use a SHA-256 fingerprint to find the real key.

  cd ~/mission6
  cat README.txt

A hash is a fingerprint for data: the same input always gives the same
hash, and changing a single letter changes it completely. You cannot
turn a hash back into the data.

The keys folder holds a dozen keys that all look alike. README.txt gives
the SHA-256 fingerprint of the real one.

Useful commands: sha256sum, grep
When you have the flag: submit CYBA{...}
Stuck? hint 6   (2 hints, 10 points each: 5% of this mission)
EOF
  ;;
  7) cat <<'EOF'
Mission 7: Letter detective   [Hard, 400 points]

Objective: Crack a substitution cipher with frequency analysis.

  cd ~/mission7
  cat cipher.txt

In a substitution cipher every letter is swapped for another one. There
are more possible keys than grains of sand on Earth, so trying them all
is hopeless.

But language leaves clues. freq counts the letters: in English e and t
are the most common, and the is the most common word. Test your guesses
with tr, writing solved letters in CAPITALS.

The last sentence holds a secret word and a number. Submit them as
CYBA{word-number}.

Useful commands: freq, tr
When you have the flag: submit CYBA{...}
Stuck? hint 7   (3 hints, 20 points each: 5% of this mission)
EOF
  ;;
  8) cat <<'EOF'
Mission 8: Keyword cipher   [Hard, 400 points]

Objective: Find a hidden keyword, then decrypt a Vigenère cipher.

  cd ~/mission8
  cat poem.txt

A Vigenère cipher is a Caesar shift that changes with every letter,
following a keyword: with the key cab, the first letter shifts by 2, the
next by 0, the next by 1, then the key repeats.

secret.txt was encrypted with a keyword. The keyword is hidden in plain
sight in poem.txt. Find it, then decrypt.

Useful commands: vigenere, cat
When you have the flag: submit CYBA{...}
Stuck? hint 8   (2 hints, 20 points each: 5% of this mission)
EOF
  ;;
  9) cat <<'EOF'
Mission 9: The vault   [Very Hard, 800 points]

Objective: Recover the keyword, then decode and decrypt the vault.

  cd ~/mission9
  cat README.txt

The final mission chains everything. vault.b64 was encrypted with a
Vigenère cipher, then encoded in base64.

The keyword is written in key.txt, but that note was itself scrambled
with a Caesar shift. Recover the keyword, decode the vault, decrypt it.

Useful commands: caesar, base64, vigenere
When you have the flag: submit CYBA{...}
Stuck? hint 9   (4 hints, 40 points each: 5% of this mission)
EOF
  ;;
  *) cat <<'EOF'
Your missions:
   0  Warm-up (optional)           Bonus       50 pts  (bonus, untimed)
   1  Base camp                    Easy       100 pts
   2  Hex marks the spot           Easy       100 pts
   3  Spin cycle                   Easy       100 pts
   4  Hail Caesar                  Medium     200 pts
   5  Russian doll                 Medium     200 pts
   6  Fingerprints                 Medium     200 pts
   7  Letter detective             Hard       400 pts
   8  Keyword cipher               Hard       400 pts
   9  The vault                    Very Hard  800 pts
                                             2500 pts

Read one with: mission 1   (up to 9)
EOF
  ;;
esac
